Below is a **step‑by‑step recipe** for adding a hillshade layer that is driven by a **raster‑DEM** source to a MapLibre GL JS map.  
It works with any style (your own JSON, a MapLibre demo style, or a style you build program‑matically) and does **not** require the newer “terrain” API – the hillshade renderer works directly on the DEM tiles.

---

## 1. Prepare the DEM tiles

| What you need | Typical options |
|---------------|-----------------|
| **Raster‑DEM tiles** (GeoTIFF‑like data that has elevation values) | • OpenMapTiles DEM (`https://tiles.openmaptiles.org/terrain/{z}/{x}/{y}.pbf`)  <br>• MapTiler DEM (`https://api.maptiler.com/tiles/terrain/{z}/{x}/{y}.png?key=YOUR_KEY`)  <br>• Your own Cloud‑Optimized GeoTIFF (COG) served via a tile server (e.g. `tileserver-gl`, `tegola`, `tileserver-php`) |
| **Tile format** | Must be a raster format that MapLibre can read (`png`, `jpg`, `webp`, or `pbf` if you use Mapbox’s “terrain‑rgb” tiles). |
| **Tile size** | 256 px (default) or 512 px – just keep it consistent with the `tileSize` you declare in the source. |
| **CORS** | The tile server must send `Access‑Control‑Allow‑Origin: *` (or your domain) so the browser can fetch the tiles. |

> **Tip:** If you already have a *terrain‑rgb* source (the Mapbox “terrain‑rgb” tiles that encode elevation in the RGB channels), you can use it directly – MapLibre will decode the elevation for you.

---

## 2. Add the raster‑DEM source to the map

You can do this **inside the style JSON** or **via the runtime API** (`map.addSource`).  
Both approaches are shown; pick the one that fits your workflow.

### 2.1. In‑style JSON (static style)

```json
{
  "version": 8,
  "sources": {
    "dem": {
      "type": "raster-dem",
      "tiles": [
        "https://tiles.openmaptiles.org/terrain/{z}/{x}/{y}.png"
      ],
      "tileSize": 256,
      "maxzoom": 14,
      "attribution": "© OpenMapTiles"
    }
  },
  "layers": [
    /* other layers … */
  ]
}
```

### 2.2. Runtime API (dynamic addition)

```js
// Assuming `map` is an already‑initialized maplibregl.Map instance
map.addSource('dem', {
  type: 'raster-dem',
  tiles: [
    'https://tiles.openmaptiles.org/terrain/{z}/{x}/{y}.png'
  ],
  tileSize: 256,
  maxzoom: 14,
  // optional: give the source a name for debugging
  attribution: '© OpenMapTiles'
});
```

> **Why `raster-dem`?**  
> The `raster-dem` source tells MapLibre that the raster tiles contain *elevation* data, not just colour. This enables the hillshade renderer (and the terrain API) to read the values and compute shading on the fly.

---

## 3. Add the hillshade layer

A hillshade layer is a **special layer type** (`"type": "hillshade"`).  
It must reference the DEM source you just added.

### 3.1. In‑style JSON

```json
{
  "id": "hillshade",
  "type": "hillshade",
  "source": "dem",
  "layout": {},
  "paint": {
    // Optional – tweak the look
    "hillshade-illumination-direction": 335,   // degrees clockwise from north
    "hillshade-illumination-anchor": "map",    // or "viewport"
    "hillshade-exaggeration": 0.5,            // 0 = flat, 1 = normal, >1 = more contrast
    "hillshade-shadow-color": "#000000",
    "hillshade-highlight-color": "#ffffff",
    "hillshade-accent-color": "#777777"
  }
}
```

### 3.2. Runtime API (add after the map is loaded)

```js
map.on('load', () => {
  // 1️⃣ Add the source (if you haven’t already done it in the style)
  map.addSource('dem', {
    type: 'raster-dem',
    tiles: ['https://tiles.openmaptiles.org/terrain/{z}/{x}/{y}.png'],
    tileSize: 256,
    maxzoom: 14
  });

  // 2️⃣ Add the hillshade layer
  map.addLayer({
    id: 'hillshade',
    type: 'hillshade',
    source: 'dem',
    layout: {},               // empty object – hillshade has no layout properties
    paint: {
      // Play with these to get the visual you want
      'hillshade-illumination-direction': 315,
      'hillshade-illumination-anchor': 'map',
      'hillshade-exaggeration': 0.7,
      'hillshade-shadow-color': 'rgba(0,0,0,0.5)',
      'hillshade-highlight-color': 'rgba(255,255,255,0.5)',
      'hillshade-accent-color': 'rgba(120,120,120,0.5)'
    }
  });
});
```

> **Important:**  
> *The hillshade layer **must be added after** the DEM source is available (i.e., after the `load` event or after you call `addSource`).*  
> *If you have other raster or vector layers that you want to appear **on top** of the hillshade, add the hillshade **first** (or use the `before` argument of `addLayer`).*

---

## 4. (Optional) Add a terrain layer for 3‑D extrusion

If you also want the map surface itself to be displaced in 3‑D (e.g., for extruded buildings, fly‑throughs, or a “3‑D globe” effect), you can enable the **terrain API** on the same DEM source:

```js
map.setTerrain({ source: 'dem', exaggeration: 1.5 });
```

> **Note:**  
> *The hillshade renderer works **independently** of the terrain API. You can have hillshade **without** terrain, terrain **without** hillshade, or both together.*

---

## 5. Fine‑tuning the visual appearance

| Paint property | What it does | Typical values |
|----------------|--------------|----------------|
| `hillshade-illumination-direction` | Sun azimuth (0 = north, clockwise) | 0‑360 (e.g., 315 for a north‑west sun) |
| `hillshade-illumination-anchor` | Whether the sun direction rotates with the map (`viewport`) or stays fixed to the world (`map`) | `"map"` (default) or `"viewport"` |
| `hillshade-exaggeration` | Multiplies the elevation before shading – higher = more contrast | 0 – 2 (0 = flat, 1 = normal) |
| `hillshade-shadow-color` | Colour of the “shadowed” side | Any CSS colour, often a semi‑transparent black |
| `hillshade-highlight-color` | Colour of the “sunlit” side | Usually a light colour (white or light gray) |
| `hillshade-accent-color` | Colour of the “side‑lit” ridge lines | Often a medium gray |

Experiment in the browser console:

```js
map.setPaintProperty('hillshade', 'hillshade-exaggeration', 1.2);
map.setPaintProperty('hillshade', 'hillshade-illumination-direction', 45);
```

---

## 6. Common pitfalls & how to fix them

| Symptom | Likely cause | Fix |
|---------|--------------|-----|
| **No hillshade appears** (map stays flat) | • DEM source URL is wrong or blocked by CORS.<br>• `type` is mistakenly set to `"raster"` instead of `"raster-dem"`.<br>• Layer added before source is ready. | • Verify the tile URL works in a browser (open a tile URL directly).<br>• Check network tab for `Access‑Control‑Allow‑Origin` header.<br>• Use `map.on('load', …)` or `map.once('sourcedata', …)` before adding the layer. |
| **Hillshade looks completely white or black** | • `hillshade-exaggeration` set to 0.<br>• `hillshade-shadow-color` / `highlight-color` set to the same value.<br>• DEM tiles contain only 0 elevation (e.g., a placeholder). | • Set `hillshade-exaggeration` > 0 (e.g., 0.5).<br>• Use contrasting shadow/highlight colours.<br>• Confirm the DEM actually contains elevation (open a tile in a GIS viewer). |
| **CORS error in console** | • Tile server missing `Access‑Control‑Allow‑Origin` header. | • Add the header on the server side (e.g., `add_header Access-Control-Allow-Origin *;` in Nginx).<br>• Proxy the tiles through a CORS‑enabled endpoint. |
| **Hillshade flickers when zooming** | • DEM tiles are being re‑requested at each zoom because `maxzoom` is too low. | • Set `maxzoom` on the source to at least the highest zoom you’ll display (e.g., 14 or 15). |
| **Hillshade looks wrong for CJK regions** | • You’re also loading a `localIdeographFontFamily` that hides the hillshade under a huge label layer. | • Adjust layer order (`map.moveLayer('hillshade', 'road-label')`). |

---

## 7. Full minimal example (HTML + JS)

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8" />
  <title>MapLibre – Hillshade from raster‑DEM</title>
  <script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />
  <style>
    body, html { margin:0; padding:0; height:100%; }
    #map { width:100%; height:100%; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>
<script>
  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: {
      version: 8,
      sources: {
        // Base map (you can replace this with any vector source you like)
        osm: {
          type: 'vector',
          url: 'https://basemaps.cartocdn.com/rastertiles/voyager/{z}/{x}/{y}.png',
          // For a vector source you would use a proper vector tiles URL.
        },
        // DEM source – change the URL to your own tiles if you have them
        dem: {
          type: 'raster-dem',
          tiles: [
            'https://tiles.openmaptiles.org/terrain/{z}/{x}/{y}.png'
          ],
          tileSize: 256,
          maxzoom: 14
        }
      },
      layers: [
        // Simple raster base layer (optional)
        {
          id: 'basemap',
          type: 'raster',
          source: 'osm',
          paint: {}
        },
        // Hillshade layer – will be drawn on top of the base map
        {
          id: 'hillshade',
          type: 'hillshade',
          source: 'dem',
          paint: {
            'hillshade-illumination-direction': 315,
            'hillshade-exaggeration': 0.7,
            'hillshade-shadow-color': 'rgba(0,0,0,0.5)',
            'hillshade-highlight-color': 'rgba(255,255,255,0.5)'
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    center: [0, 0],
    zoom: 2,
    // Optional: improve CJK rendering (does not affect hillshade)
    localIdeographFontFamily: 'sans-serif'
  });

  // Optional: enable 3‑D terrain as well
  // map.on('load', () => map.setTerrain({ source: 'dem', exaggeration: 1.5 }));
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

Open the file in a browser, and you should see a shaded relief over the world map. Adjust the `paint` values to suit your design.

---

## 8. Quick checklist before shipping

1. **DEM source URL** – reachable, CORS‑enabled, correct `tileSize`.
2. **Source type** – `raster-dem` (not `raster`).
3. **Layer type** – `hillshade`.
4. **Layer order** – placed **below** any label or symbol layers you want to stay on top.
5. **Paint properties** – set at least `hillshade-exaggeration` (default 1) if you want visible relief.
6. **Test at multiple zoom levels**
